#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8185f86d-cee9-525e-abc2-3367b6a93654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057213} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c6b1be-0769-5621-8554-6ca01947b135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057214} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e54359-5262-5cd7-bb38-abc8a86788bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057215} {:answer/id #uuid "63f23a08-311b-53db-bab0-9202bedbb0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057216} {:answer/id #uuid "65772919-94fb-51b0-8f90-ba3e80544619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8185f86d-cee9-525e-abc2-3367b6a93654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057213} :answer-field/id #uuid "c706cddc-178c-5574-b93d-10eaa9f26fde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "882c4c5b-7c6a-5276-989d-a6c4749ae87b" :question/math-academy-id "q-18280" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =5|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5(0-2)+0+3(-2+3) \\\\  & =-10+0+3 \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{33}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{33}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{33} & =(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-15-0 \\\\  & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{33}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{33}=\\frac{1}{-7}\\cdot (-15)=\\frac{15}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c704e019-035f-5807-b02d-62b9cd44c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Element of the Inverse of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4055c931-9c69-5293-9813-b782ee0735a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5ebfe8-4222-5d74-911a-9ab1f9988bcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279517} {:answer/id #uuid "72785da6-d489-5693-8457-2c0ca9a138bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279518} {:answer/id #uuid "dd74a7fb-9fb1-5ae8-908b-5e9a223aa2ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{44}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279519} {:answer/id #uuid "04ee1b6b-8b93-5f8c-ba7d-55eb490d6b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4055c931-9c69-5293-9813-b782ee0735a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279516} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ebd8d4-01ee-506c-b9cf-53cda8dd55f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5434732d-193f-528d-b9d5-29d58f90d985" :question/math-academy-id "q-18281" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|-4|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(0+9)-4(0-6)+1(0+2) \\\\  & =18+24+2 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{44}\\cdot 6=\\frac{3}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3262839-4028-5d90-bf8a-cb4de958813a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279523} {:answer/id #uuid "0e326d50-6309-5e63-ba00-a5eb1f4e96b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e326d50-6309-5e63-ba00-a5eb1f4e96b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279524} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d153166-8679-52de-a7b3-11c4606fe3fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "39eba7b4-55f1-5b6e-bfe4-784dcc986294" :question/math-academy-id "q-319437" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=k-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=k-1\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1+2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k-1}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{k-1} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k-1 & =-2 \\\\ k & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e08e64f2-ae68-5bc9-9404-8f04dfbcc2eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e08e64f2-ae68-5bc9-9404-8f04dfbcc2eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279527} :answer-field/id #uuid "77609ad9-1e1e-5605-8bac-5fe1a74641a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0b992d3-0ba8-54d4-b374-4ea9b116d69e" :question/math-academy-id "q-319442" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $det(A)=-2k-3.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-2k-3\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{23}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}^{t}={c}_{32}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{32} & =(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(1+2) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{23}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2k-3)}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{23}^{′}=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\,2k+3\\,} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 12 & =2k+3 \\\\ 2k & =9 \\\\ k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d79fb5d1-22a6-511d-90ca-7d66c540d172" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cofactor Method To Solve for an Unknown Quantity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a48cbc76-7cda-5abf-93b1-80a129d8f198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059246} {:answer/id #uuid "c4410ebd-d421-55a6-beb3-b0a3c10f8b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059247} {:answer/id #uuid "cd833193-5489-55be-90cd-60e2e7bcd7e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059248} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2a9f22-301d-5015-80cf-b0fbebf56608" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059249} {:answer/id #uuid "53467d29-9f84-51c9-be76-f602dc94f0dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4410ebd-d421-55a6-beb3-b0a3c10f8b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059247} :answer-field/id #uuid "adece073-b29c-5ffa-b95a-f1c279d3f84f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "20571cf0-076d-5c7b-a788-d7a5f5cb703e" :question/math-academy-id "q-9710" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ k & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\nequals $18.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ k & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ k & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ k & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-24)-2(0-4k)+3(0-k) \\\\  & =8k-3k-24 \\\\  & =5k-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0-18) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5k-24}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5k-24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=18$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{5k-24} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5k-24} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =5k-24 \\\\ 25 & =5k \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
